 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we know the tablets were broken on the seventeenth of Tammuz?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "The reckoning continues in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "taanit.28b.8",
   "daf": "28b",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "חֲמִשָּׁה דְּבָרִים אֵירְעוּ אֶת אֲבוֹתֵינוּ בְּשִׁבְעָה עָשָׂר בְּתַמּוּז וְכוּ׳. נִשְׁתַּבְּרוּ הַלּוּחוֹת, מְנָלַן? דְּתַנְיָא: בְּשִׁשָּׁה לַחֹדֶשׁ נִיתְּנוּ עֲשֶׂרֶת הַדִּבְּרוֹת לְיִשְׂרָאֵל, רַבִּי יוֹסֵי אוֹמֵר: בְּשִׁבְעָה בּוֹ. מַאן דְּאָמַר בְּשִׁשָּׁה נִיתְּנוּ — בְּשִׁשָּׁה נִיתְּנוּ, וּבְשִׁבְעָה עָלָה מֹשֶׁה.",
   "en": "§ The mishna taught: «Five» calamitous «matters occurred to our forefathers on the seventeenth of Tammuz,» one of which was that «the tablets were broken.» The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive that the tablets were broken on this day? «As it is taught» in a baraita: «On the sixth of the month» of Sivan «the Ten Commandments were given to the Jewish people. Rabbi Yosei says:» It was «on the seventh of that» month. The Gemara comments: According to «the one who said» that «they were given on the sixth» of Sivan, «they were given on the sixth, and on the seventh Moses ascended» to Mount Sinai."
  }
 ],
